CONTEXT: We are porting this C++ program to pure Rust (2021 edition, std only, ZERO external crates):

    #include "clipper2/clipper.h"
    using namespace Clipper2Lib;
    int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
        Path64 rect1 = {Point64(0,0), Point64(50,0), Point64(50,50), Point64(0,50)};
        Path64 rect2 = {Point64(25,25), Point64(75,25), Point64(75,75), Point64(25,75)};
        Path64 rect3 = {Point64(50,50), Point64(100,50), Point64(100,100), Point64(50,100)};
        Paths64 subjects = {rect1, rect2, rect3};
        Paths64 unionAll = Union(subjects, FillRule::NonZero);
        double totalArea = 0;
        for (const auto& path : unionAll) totalArea += Area(path);
        Paths64 translated = TranslatePaths(unionAll, 200, 200);
        std::cout << totalArea << std::endl;
        return 0;
    }

The compiled C++ binary prints exactly the 5 bytes "6250\n" for EVERY argument list (args are ignored), exit code 0.

HARD RULES for you:
- BLACK BOX ONLY. Do NOT read, fetch, or quote the real Clipper2 C++/C# source. It is not on disk. Infer semantics from the public interface and from general computational-geometry knowledge.
- The Rust port must use ONLY the standard library. No crates.io dependencies.
- Target toolchain is rustc 1.75.0 / cargo 1.75.0, edition 2021. Do not propose APIs newer than 1.75.
- Your final message IS the return value. Return data, not chit-chat.


You are one of three independent architects. Design the Rust implementation of the polygon boolean engine using YOUR ASSIGNED APPROACH ONLY. Do not hedge toward the others.

Approach C: Martinez-Rueda-Trecu sweep-line boolean operation.
Sweep events (left/right endpoints + intersection-split events) in a priority queue, a status line of segments ordered by y-at-x, computeFields to derive inOut/otherInOut/inResult flags, then result-segment connection into contours with hole/parent tracking.

Requirements the design must satisfy:
- Pure std Rust, edition 2021, rustc 1.75. Integer coordinates are i64. No unsafe required.
- Must produce, for the 3-rectangle NonZero union above, exactly one outer ring of signed area +6250 (total 6250).
- Must correctly handle these degeneracies present in the input: a pure point-touch between rect1 and rect3 at (50,50); T-junctions where a rectangle corner lies in the interior of another rectangle's edge (e.g. (50,25) on rect1's right edge x=50, (25,50) on rect1's top edge y=50, (75,50)/(50,75) similarly); COLLINEAR OVERLAPPING edges (rect1's right edge x=50 from y=25..50 lies on rect3's left edge x=50 from y=50..100? verify which segments actually overlap) ; and vertices where 3+ edges meet.
- Output convention: outer contours counter-clockwise (positive shoelace area in a Y-up frame), holes clockwise (negative), no duplicated closing vertex, no collinear redundant vertices, deterministic ordering.
- Must generalise: arbitrary simple/self-intersecting input rings, all four fill rules, and the other boolean ops (Intersect / Difference / Xor).
- Must avoid i64 overflow in cross products: state exactly where i128 is required.

Here is the ground-truth geometry spec produced by another agent (trust but verify):
{"unionPolygons":[{"vertices":[[0,0],[50,0],[50,25],[75,25],[75,50],[100,50],[100,100],[50,100],[50,75],[25,75],[25,50],[0,50]],"signedArea":6250,"isHole":false}],"totalArea":6250,"reasoning":"GROUND TRUTH: exactly ONE output ring, 12 vertices, no holes, signed area +6250. Union({rect1,rect2,rect3}, NonZero) is a simply-connected rectilinear \"staircase\" polygon. Clipper2's Union with a single subject set returns Paths64 of size 1 here, so `for (path : unionAll) totalArea += Area(path)` sums a single +6250 and `std::cout << 6250.0` prints \"6250\" (default %g, 4 sig digits, no decimal point) then endl -> the 5 bytes \"6250\\n\".\n\n== 1. WHY NO HOLE AND WHY (50,50) IS NOT ON THE BOUNDARY ==\nThe whole subtlety of this input is the rect1/rect3 point-contact at (50,50). It is NOT a boundary pinch, because an open disk of radius 25 around (50,50) lies inside rect2 = [25,75]x[25,75]. Winding numbers of the four quadrant faces meeting at (50,50) (probe points, verified numerically):\n  (49.5,49.5) SW: rect1+rect2         -> w = 2\n  (50.5,49.5) SE: rect2 only          -> w = 1\n  (50.5,50.5) NE: rect2+rect3         -> w = 2\n  (49.5,50.5) NW: rect2 only          -> w = 1\nAll four are nonzero under NonZero, so (50,50) is an interior point of the union and must NOT appear as an output vertex. A naive engine that treats coincident vertices from two rings as a boundary node emits a spurious pinch / zero-area spike / two rings there.\nComplement check for holes: the two non-covered pockets are {[0,25]x[50,100]} u {[25,50]x[75,100]} (NW) and {[50,100]x[0,25]} u {[75,100]x[25,50]} (SE). Both reach the unbounded exterior, so the complement is connected -> no holes -> exactly one ring, positive.\n\n== 2. BOUNDARY TRACE (CCW in a Y-up math frame) ==\nStart (0,0), east along y=0 (rect1 bottom) to (50,0); north along x=50 to (50,25) where rect2's bottom edge y=25 takes over; east along y=25 to (75,25); north along x=75 to (75,50) where rect3's bottom edge y=50 takes over; east along y=50 to (100,50); north along x=100 to (100,100); west along y=100 to (50,100); south along x=50 to (50,75) where rect2's top edge y=75 takes over; west along y=75 to (25,75); south along x=25 to (25,50) where rect1's top edge y=50 takes over; west along y=50 to (0,50); south along x=0 back to (0,0).\nRing (canonical, rotation-invariant; Clipper2 may start at any vertex, area is unaffected):\n  (0,0) (50,0) (50,25) (75,25) (75,50) (100,50) (100,100) (50,100) (50,75) (25,75) (25,50) (0,50)\nNo three consecutive vertices are collinear, so this is also the form Clipper2 emits with the default PreserveCollinear=false.\n\n== 3. SHOELACE ARITHMETIC (2A = sum of x_i*y_{i+1} - x_{i+1}*y_i) ==\n  (  0,  0)->( 50,  0):   0*0   -  50*0   =     0 -     0 =      0   run 2A =     0\n  ( 50,  0)->( 50, 25):  50*25  -  50*0   =  1250 -     0 =  +1250   run 2A =  1250\n  ( 50, 25)->( 75, 25):  50*25  -  75*25  =  1250 -  1875 =   -625   run 2A =   625\n  ( 75, 25)->( 75, 50):  75*50  -  75*25  =  3750 -  1875 =  +1875   run 2A =  2500\n  ( 75, 50)->(100, 50):  75*50  - 100*50  =  3750 -  5000 =  -1250   run 2A =  1250\n  (100, 50)->(100,100): 100*100 - 100*50  = 10000 -  5000 =  +5000   run 2A =  6250\n  (100,100)->( 50,100): 100*100 -  50*100 = 10000 -  5000 =  +5000   run 2A = 11250\n  ( 50,100)->( 50, 75):  50*75  -  50*100 =  3750 -  5000 =  -1250   run 2A = 10000\n  ( 50, 75)->( 25, 75):  50*75  -  25*75  =  3750 -  1875 =  +1875   run 2A = 11875\n  ( 25, 75)->( 25, 50):  25*50  -  25*75  =  1250 -  1875 =   -625   run 2A = 11250\n  ( 25, 50)->(  0, 50):  25*50  -   0*50  =  1250 -     0 =  +1250   run 2A = 12500\n  (  0, 50)->(  0,  0):   0*0   -   0*50  =     0 -     0 =      0   run 2A = 12500\n  2A = 12500  =>  signedArea = +6250.  Positive => outer contour, CCW in Y-up (= Clipper2's positive/outer orientation, since Area() there is plain shoelace/2 and the harness prints +6250).\n  totalArea = +6250. VERIFIED = 6250.\n\n== 4. THREE INDEPENDENT CROSS-CHECKS ==\n(a) Inclu

And the API contract spec:
{"findings":["PROGRAM CONTRACT (the only observable): the binary ignores argc/argv entirely, writes exactly the 5 bytes `36 32 35 30 0a` (\"6250\\n\") to stdout, nothing to stderr, and exits 0. Verified by running /workspace/dataset/test6_executable with 0 and 3 args. Source is /workspace/dataset/test6.cpp; the `translated` result of TranslatePaths is computed but never printed (it exists only to force the call to be made and must not abort/panic).","Point64: value struct with two signed 64-bit fields, `int64_t x, y` (a third `int64_t z` exists only under the USINGZ build flag; the test build does not use it, so the Rust port should be `#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Hash, Debug, Default)] pub struct Point64 { pub x: i64, pub y: i64 }`). Constructors: default -> (0,0); `Point64(int64_t x, int64_t y)`; a template ctor from any arithmetic pair that ROUNDS when the source is floating point (round-half-to-even via nearbyint, not half-away-from-zero). Public ops: `==`/`!=` compare x and y componentwise (z ignored even when present), `+`, `-` (binary and unary negate), `*` by a scalar, and `operator<<` printing \"x,y \".","Coordinate range guard: Clipper2 defines MAX_COORD = INT64_MAX/2 = 4_611_686_018_427_387_903 (~4.6116860184e18) and MIN_COORD = -MAX_COORD; INVALID = INT64_MAX is used as a sentinel. The value is exactly INT64_MAX/2 because the internal shoelace/cross-product code adds two coordinates (y_i + y_{i+1}) and subtracts two (x_i - x_{i+1}) as raw int64 arithmetic - halving the range makes both provably non-overflowing. Clipper2 range-checks coordinates entering the clipping engine and raises a Clipper2Exception / range_error (\"Coordinate outside allowed range\") when |x| or |y| > MAX_COORD. Port as `pub const MAX_COORD: i64 = i64::MAX / 2; pub const MIN_COORD: i64 = -MAX_COORD;` with a checked entry point; unobservable for this test (all coords are 0..100).","Path64 = `std::vector<Point64>`. It is an IMPLICITLY CLOSED ring: the edge from `path[len-1]` back to `path[0]` is implied and the first point must NOT be repeated at the end. Order of points defines orientation. Empty and 1/2-point paths are legal values (degenerate, zero area). Rust: `pub type Path64 = Vec<Point64>;`.","Paths64 = `std::vector<Path64>`, an unordered-by-contract collection of rings. Rust: `pub type Paths64 = Vec<Path64>;`. Both Path64 and Paths64 are plain vectors, so `{a, b, c}` brace-init in the test is just a 3-element vector of rings, in the given order: rect1, rect2, rect3.","FillRule is `enum class FillRule { EvenOdd, NonZero, Positive, Negative };` - exactly four enumerators, in that order, with implicit discriminants 0,1,2,3 (EvenOdd is the default in most Clipper2 wrappers). Rust: `#[derive(Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Debug)] pub enum FillRule { EvenOdd = 0, NonZero = 1, Positive = 2, Negative = 3 }`.","Winding predicates (w = signed winding number of a region, computed from all contributing edges with left-to-right/downward crossings signed by edge direction; a CCW ring in a Y-up frame contributes +1): EvenOdd -> inside iff (w & 1) != 0, i.e. |w| is odd; NonZero -> inside iff w != 0; Positive -> inside iff w > 0; Negative -> inside iff w < 0. The rule is applied to the subject set and (independently, with the same rule) to the clip set before the boolean op combines them.","Area(const Path64&) -> double is the shoelace sum: `0.5 * SUM_over_i (y_i + y_{i+1}) * (x_i - x_{i+1})`, indices modulo n so the implied closing edge is included. This is algebraically identical to `0.5 * SUM (x_i*y_{i+1} - x_{i+1}*y_i)` (the x_i*y_i terms telescope away over a closed loop). The C++ accumulates in `double`: each term is `(double)(int64 y_i + int64 y_{i+1}) * (double)(int64 x_i - int64 x_{i+1})`, i.e. the add/subtract are int64 and only the product and accumulation are double. Iteration order starts with the closing edge (last,first) then (0,1),(1,2),...,(n-2,n-1) - reproduce that order in Rust for bit-identical float accumulation.","Area sign convention: POSITIVE for a counter-clockwise ring in a Y-up (mathematical) frame; negative for clockwise. Clipper2's documentation states the same fact as \"positive = clockwise\" because it assumes a Y-DOWN screen frame - it is the same arithmetic, only the visual interpretation flips. Concretely, `Path64{(0,0),(50,0),(50,50),(0,50)}` (the test's rect1) has Area == +2500.0 exactly. Do not take an absolute value anywhere.","Area(path) returns exactly 0.0 when path.size() < 3 (checked before any arithmetic; covers empty, 1-point and 2-point paths). Rust must early-return 0.0 rather than index/wrap. Note the shoelace of a 2-point path is 0 anyway, but 0- and 1-point paths would panic on modular indexing.","Area(const Paths64&) -> double sums Area(p) for every p in iteration order, accumulating in a single `double`; returns 0.0 for an empty Paths64. Both Area overloads are templates over coordinate type (Path64 and PathD both supported); the port only needs the i64 flavour plus optionally an f64 flavour.","Union has two free-function overloads with DIFFERENT argument orders - do not conflate them: `Paths64 Union(const Paths64& subjects, FillRule fillrule)` (2-arg, no clips; this is the one the test calls) and `Paths64 Union(const Paths64& subjects, const Paths64& clips, FillRule fillrule)` (3-arg, fill rule LAST). PathD variants take a trailing `int precision = 2`. Rust: `pub fn union_paths(subjects: &Paths64, fill_rule: FillRule) -> Paths64` and `pub fn union_with_clips(subjects: &Paths64, clips: &Paths64, fill_rule: FillRule) -> Paths64`.","Union(subjects, fillrule) is exactly `BooleanOp(ClipType::Union, fillrule, subjects, Paths64{})`: every input path is added as a CLOSED SUBJECT polygon, the clip set is empty, and no open (polyline) paths are involved. With an empty clip set, Union degenerates to \"self-union / normalize\": it resolves self-intersections and mutual overlaps of the subject set under the given fill rule.","BooleanOp signature and enum: `Paths64 Boolea

Deliver a design detailed enough that an implementer writes it without further research: give the actual predicates (cross products, orientation tests), the actual intersection-rounding rule, the exact data structures with Rust type signatures, and the tie-breaking rules at each ordering comparison. Be brutally specific about the point-touch at (50,50): say what your algorithm outputs there and why that still totals 6250.

